Here some details about font handling.

Fonts are in .txf format, see
http://www.opengl.org/resources/code/samples/mjktips/TexFont/TexFont.html
for details. Some discussion about font formats is on:
http://www.sjbaker.org/steve/omniv/opengl_text.html

I included the 'showtxf' sources in this directory

There are (for now) two ways to convert a ttf font to txf:
1) a) convert ttf to a X-windows font (fontforge is able to do that,
      see  http://fontforge.sourceforge.net/ - read the FAQ on how to create
      bitmaps)
   b) Install the font on your system
   c) convert the X-windows font to txf via gentexfont (see link above)
   --> I had only limited success with this approach, the fonts were too
       blocky. By specifying the right parameters it should be possible
       to get better quality.

   Note: while it might be possible to directly install ttf fonts, I wasn't
   able to load a ttf font in gentexfont.

2) Following the instruction from the font.pl file by using an afm file
   (see README.font for the original README).
   a) export GS_FONTPATH=<wherever the font is>
   b) ttf2afm yourfont.ttf > whatever.afm
      ttf2afm is part of the pdf2tex package, see
      http://www.tug.org/applications/pdftex (the README.font says it's
      part of ImageMagick. But I couldn't find it in that package).
   c) ./font.pl whatever.afm
   The export IS important, otherwise a system default font will be used when
   creating the txf font, not your ttf font (without any error message or
   warning)
   When I am using this approach, some of the characters are being chopped
   off at the top or at the bottom (e.g. 'f', 'g', '{', ...).

For now the .txf file is only ASCII, i.e. not latin1 special characters >127
are available, work on adding the missing latin-1 characters (which are
part of the ttf file) is in progress.

================================================================================
   
The font used in STK race gui (i.e. not in the menu) is
'Domestic_Manners.ttf', and can be found at:
http://www.1001fonts.com/font_details.html?font_id=2450
It's freeware, released under GPL.

I renamed the file to be DomesticManners.ttf, since this way the Makefile
would work better (since the .txf file is using the name of the font which
is 'DomesticManners', and not the filename 'Domestic_Manner').

================================================================================

I wrote a very simple makefile, which will create the showtex binary, 
and the DomesticManners.txf file. I included the .afm file as well, since
it might be a bit more work to create (install pdftex).

   hiker

